USC: don't merge or reorder loads across stores to the same memory

MergeIdenticalInstructions replaced a load by an identical earlier one even
when a store in between could have changed the memory, and the dependency
graph only ordered loads and stores after earlier stores, so a store could
be moved before an earlier load of the same address.  Reading a value back
after storing to it (OpenCL local atomics, which the front end lowers to a
load, an operation and a store, or stores through pointers which may alias)
gave the old value.  Start a new set of merge candidates for memory loads at
each store, lock, release or atomic load, and order a store after earlier
loads it may alias.  The disambiguation also compared the static offsets of
loads in different units from stores' and only kept the order when the
store's range was inside the access's; compare byte ranges for overlap.

